## QNX System Profiler Analysis

A kernel event trace was captured on the Raspberry Pi 4 while the supervisor ran and faults were injected.

### Summary

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Trace length | 17.4 s |
| Trace events captured | 741,143, with 0 dropped buffers |
| CPU idle | 99.0 % |
| User time | 0.3 % |
| Kernel time | 0.7 % |

The whole framework, including the supervisor, three ECUs and dashboard, leaves the system about 99 % idle. A safety monitor should add very little load to the functions it protects.

c:\Users\manya\OneDrive\Pictures\Screenshots\Screenshot 2026-10-31 002146.png

### CPU Usage

CPU load is very low because the supervisor is event-driven. It wakes only on the 100 ms timer pulse or when a heartbeat pulse arrives, and does not poll in a busy loop.

c:\Users\manya\OneDrive\Pictures\Screenshots\Screenshot 2026-10-31 004921.png

### Thread Timeline

Each tick mark is a heartbeat. The `pi_supervisor` row keeps ticking throughout, because it is the watchdog. Engine and Infotainment keep their regular ticks without a gap.

In the Brakes deadlock run, the first `pi_brakes_ecu` row stops ticking when the heartbeat stops. After the supervisor detects the fault and restarts the process, a second `pi_brakes_ecu` row starts ticking. QNX shows the restarted process as a new entry, because it has a new process ID.

